Subject: [PATCH 1/2] dsh: a patch list mid-write is left to the next round
 (#5782)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

dsh's desktop rewrites its own cordis.patch.yml in place — truncate,
then the body — so for a moment the file is its head alone: magpie's
route entries sit up front, and a round that read in that window saw a
wired list of two and wrote that half-state back as the whole list. The
user's other entries were gone with it, over and over (stevenpisces: 57
entries became 2, dsh's first-setup marker with them, and the desktop's
own save could not bring them back while the rounds kept at it).

A fresh list is reread after a beat; one that moved under us is left to
the next round, which reads it whole. A list whose writer is done
between the two reads costs the round a beat and nothing else; the
rounds run seconds apart, so the route lands one round later than it
did, and lands whole.

Ablation: with the reread out, TestDshRouteAgainSkipsAFileBeingWritten
fails at "the half-state was fixed as the list" — the user's entries
gone, the reporter's shape. Whole, the test passes: the body lands
inside the beat, the round skips the half, and the next round reads
every entry.

Windows itself wasn't at hand (darwin): the half-write runs as a
fixture against the same parsers the rounds read, and the desktop's
writer is played by a delayed second write; a real Windows dsh desktop
mid-save is NOT-RUNNABLE here.

Run at upstream/main b27b66046 + this commit, macOS 15 (darwin/arm64):
internal/agent whole ok, TestDsh family -race ok; the one red in the
package is TestMothX, red on the parent commit too (pre-existing).

